\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces A hook walk on $\mitlambda =(5,3,3,1)$; the cells show hook lengths and the three corners are shaded. At each step the walk jumps to a uniformly chosen other cell of the current hook. This particular walk has probability $\frac  17\cdot \frac  13=\frac  1{21}$. Over all starting cells, the corners $(1,5)$, $(3,3)$, $(4,1)$ are reached with total weights $\frac  {24}{7},\frac  {20}{3},\frac  {40}{21}$, which add up to $n=12$.}}{3}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hl_walk}{{2}{3}{A hook walk on $\lambda =(5,3,3,1)$; the cells show hook lengths and the three corners are shaded. At each step the walk jumps to a uniformly chosen other cell of the current hook. This particular walk has probability $\frac 17\cdot \frac 13=\frac 1{21}$. Over all starting cells, the corners $(1,5)$, $(3,3)$, $(4,1)$ are reached with total weights $\frac {24}{7},\frac {20}{3},\frac {40}{21}$, which add up to $n=12$}{figure.caption.2}{}}
